// POSIX regular expressions, bound to the C library the machine already has.
//
// There is no `dependencies` block because this package depends on nothing sysl could fetch: the
// implementation is `regcomp`, `regexec`, `regerror` and `regfree`, which POSIX has specified since
// 1003.2 and which every hosted C library carries.
//
// This is the block a *consumer* writes to reach this package, and resolution for it is built
// (`design/packages.md` §§ 3-6): a `sysl build` fetches the coordinate, selects by MVS, checks what
// arrived against `sysl.sum`, and compiles it as more modules.
//
//     dependencies {
//       regex { git = "github.com/sysl-lang/regex", version = "0.2.1" }
//     }
//
// The coordinate is identity rather than a URL, so it carries no `https://`, and the version is the
// tag `v0.2.1` on this repository. `build-lib` and `--lib` still work and are what the tests here
// use, but they are no longer the only way in.

// `name` is the package; the module a program imports is `sh.sysl.regex`, and the two are
// deliberately different. The module carries the `sh.sysl.` prefix so that no package claims a
// top-level word another one might want; the package name is the short one, because it names this
// repository rather than a namespace.

package {
  name    = "regex"
  version = "0.2.1"
  sysl    = "0.6.0"
}

// **`posix`, because that is what `<regex.h>` is.** The four functions are not in ISO C -- they are
// in POSIX.2 -- so a target with a freestanding or non-POSIX C library does not have them. Saying so
// here turns "this cannot work there" into one refusal before anything is compiled, rather than a
// link error naming `regcomp`.
//
// **`heap`, because a `regex_t` is caller-allocated and its size is a platform's business.**
// `c/shim.c` calls `calloc` for it, and frees it again from `Regex`'s destructor -- which is the whole
// reason the handle is a pointer rather than a value this side could hold. This is the difference
// between this package and monocypher, which allocates nothing and therefore names nothing: a
// capability clause is a claim about what a library *does*.
//
// `posix` implies `os`, so that one is not written out.
//
// **What is NOT here is a `@link` directive**, and the absence is the interesting half. The other
// bindings in the organisation either carry their C (linenoise, monocypher, qcbor) or name a library
// the linker must be given (`m`, for qcbor's numeric coercions). Here the implementation is inside
// the C library itself, which the driver passes on every hosted target unasked, so naming anything
// would be naming what is already on the line (`15 §8`).
requires {
  heap  = true
  posix = true
}
